 ## Overview
-This method is a real-time, brute-force voxelization technique that determines the interiority of a voxel relative to a mesh by identifying the nearest mesh vertex and analyzing the geometric relationship between the vertex normal and the vector to the voxel [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [GPU Mesh Voxelization (Schwarz & Seidel 2010)](1633-gpu-mesh-voxelization-schwarz-seidel-2010.md) which rely on ray-casting or projection, and [Multi-channel Surface Voxelization](1899-multi-channel-surface-voxelization.md) which uses multi-sampling, by avoiding rasterization entirely in favor of direct vertex proximity checks [S1, unofficial]. The technique is primarily distinguished by its reliance on a dot-product threshold for interiority, although the source implementation notes this specific logic is potentially buggy and often superseded by distance-only checks in practice [S1, unofficial].
+This method is a real-time voxelization technique that determines if a voxel is inside, on the edge, or outside a mesh by finding the nearest vertex on the mesh for each voxel and using the dot product between the ray from the vertex to the voxel and the vertex normal to determine interiority [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [GPU Mesh Voxelization (Schwarz & Seidel 2010)](1633-gpu-mesh-voxelization-schwarz-seidel-2010.md) and [Multi-channel Surface Voxelization](1899-multi-channel-surface-voxelization.md) by avoiding traditional rasterization for the voxelization pass, instead relying on direct vertex proximity checks executed in a compute shader [S1, unofficial]. The source implementation explicitly notes that the dot-product interiority logic is considered buggy and is often superseded by distance-only checks in practice [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A mesh defined by an array of vertices (position and normal) and a voxel grid defined by an array of entities (voxel positions and scales).
-*   **Output:** A voxel grid where each voxel's scale (opacity/visibility) is updated based on its proximity to the mesh surface and interiority.
+*   **Input:** A mesh defined by an array of vertices (position and normal) and a voxel grid defined by an array of entities (voxel positions and scales) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A voxel grid where each voxel's scale (opacity/visibility) is updated based on its proximity to the mesh surface and interiority [S1, unofficial].
 
